From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 18:39:55 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] onscreen/native: Use variable refresh rate on the whole
 desktop of built-in panels

Variable refresh rate is only turned on while a fullscreen surface is on
the view. That suits external monitors, many of which flicker or shift
brightness when the refresh rate of a desktop changes. A built-in panel
that offers variable refresh rate is made to run that way all the time,
as a ProMotion style panel does on its own platform.

With VRR_ENABLED off, the kernel driver of such a panel keeps its
minimum refresh rate at the mode's nominal rate, so a window showing
low frame rate content, such as a video playing at 30 fps, makes the
panel repeat every frame at 120 Hz. With VRR_ENABLED on, the panel
waits for the next frame instead.

When a built-in output is in a variable refresh rate mode, keep variable
refresh rate on for its whole desktop, not only for fullscreen surfaces.
External outputs keep the fullscreen-only behaviour, and a built-in
output in a fixed refresh rate mode stays fixed, so choosing the fixed
mode in Settings still turns it off.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 src/backends/native/meta-onscreen-native.c | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/backends/native/meta-onscreen-native.c b/src/backends/native/meta-onscreen-native.c
index a3b40df..b90a939 100644
--- a/src/backends/native/meta-onscreen-native.c
+++ b/src/backends/native/meta-onscreen-native.c
@@ -2396,8 +2396,22 @@ maybe_update_vrr (MetaOnscreenNative *onscreen_native,
   MetaKmsUpdate *kms_update;
   gboolean vrr_enabled = FALSE;
 
+  /*
+   * Variable refresh rate normally runs only while a fullscreen surface is
+   * on the view, as many external monitors flicker or shift brightness when
+   * the refresh rate of a desktop changes. A built-in panel in a variable
+   * refresh rate mode is made to run that way all the time: let its whole
+   * desktop use it, so the panel follows the frames of windows showing low
+   * frame rate content instead of repeating them at the nominal rate.
+   */
   if (meta_output_is_vrr_enabled (onscreen_native->output))
-    vrr_enabled = onscreen_native->vrr_allowed;
+    {
+      const MetaOutputInfo *output_info =
+        meta_output_get_info (onscreen_native->output);
+
+      vrr_enabled = onscreen_native->vrr_allowed ||
+                    meta_output_info_is_builtin (output_info);
+    }
 
   if (vrr_enabled != onscreen_native->vrr_enabled)
     {
